hello steve kaufman here the linguist on  language where i  like to talk about uh language learning  and things  vaguely related to language learning and  my own views of language learning  language learning is largely a matter of  attitude i've said this many many times  attitude not aptitude  it's how you feel about yourself how you  feel about the language  the interest the confidence  the willingness to put in the time these  are all the things that will determine  whether you're going to be successful or  not  um  the other day i was reading the  newspaper here in in canada and there  was a report published  that  discovered the fact that uh they that  what they did was they they  sent out uh  answers to job postings on these big job  sites like monster.com and so forth  and what they did was they had  four more or less similar uh  applications  one one of which where the applicant had  an english-sounding lane like mary smith  or john williams  and the other three had either  obviously pakistani or  muslim names  indian names  and chinese names  but totally chinese not james wong but  wong jiang or something  and what they discovered was that um  the  applications that were  from people with obviously english names  got a 16 callback  whereas applications with these other  names foreign sounding names got 11  callback  and this was presented as a major  discovery and so forth  i don't see what's surprising about that  that to me is perfectly normal they  found that where the applicant with the  foreign sounding name  had worked in canada for a year that  increased the number of callbacks  if a person instead of putting their  name as lei lee  wrote their name as mary lee they were  also much more likely to get a call back  and this was presented as an example of  racism  or possibly racism and so forth  um  you know as someone who has been  i have hired  many immigrants in fact at any given  time 30 40 percent of my workforce is  immigrant  i have hired  three people from the people's republic  of china  uh with people from japan people from  taiwan worked with me for a long time  romania russia  i can't think of them all  um  the ability to communicate is absolutely  crucial  and i'm not talking about accents i'm  talking about the ability to communicate  comfortably  our employee from  taiwan  had very noticeable accent but had no  trouble communicating  we have an employee from japan who i  wouldn't say is a tremendous  communicator but she makes up  for it in other ways  we had an employee from romania who is a  programmer  very difficult  just difficult and it is a delight and  we have had two employees from russia  we had one from china who was impossible  and which is and he was behind me  starting up  uh what subsequently became link because  i realized that there are so many people  like him  and and so what we have in canada over  the last six ten years our labor market  has been flooded  with immigrants who are looking for work  which is normal because we have a lot of  immigrants  who are in the sort of skilled worker  category and whose english is not very  good  and  so that anybody i mean five six years  ago i was much more positive towards  hiring people from china let's say than  i am today because i have come to  realize that many of them have serious  communication problems  and anyone that's in the business of  hiring people has been  you know  had many many such experiences  uh  we once applied  for a customer service person  for link  and we wanted someone who was bilingual  in english and chinese because at that  time we were working very  hard to try to develop the chinese  market i had over 40 i think 44  applications came in  uh essentially from people who were  originally immigrants and who had gone  to university in canada and out of the  44  four  were in correct english all the others  had some kind of mistake on them  i mentioned this to a lady whom i know  who's originally from  china and who is a professor at a local  community college and i told her how  surprised i was and she said she wasn't  surprised at all  uh we have i have had uh people who are  studying at university here  uh  say to me that it's unfair  that the professor uh gives them lower  marks because of their poor english  i mean i can just say that in every  survey i have seen  of what employers are looking for they  are looking for people who can  communicate well and i repeat this  doesn't mean the accent the accent is a  non-event in my opinion  we have an employee now who's uh russian  he never gets he never gets the articles  like to him  the an  end doesn't exist  but he communicates  very well as did the person we had from  taiwan who moved on to another job he  was with us for 10 years  accent  misses the odd little nicety of the  language but communication first class  unfortunately that is not always the  case in fact often not the case with  immigrants so it is not at all  surprising that potential employers  would react this way they might have  been more favorable  favorably disposed five six years ago  now is this discrimination yeah it's  discrimination based on the experience  of the employer  he discriminates or she discriminates  and i would imagine because this survey  took place in toronto where 50 of the  population roughly is foreign born 50 of  the population is quote a visible  minority there's a very high  chance that a good number of the people  who were looking at these applications  were in fact themselves either born  outside the country or visible  minorities but they still know that  whoever they get to work in their  company has to be able to fit in  so  um  you know and i also object to the fact  that all these government organizations  they get money to do studies to show  that there's great discrimination this  is unfair what  how is that what you know what is the  impact of that on the one hand  the immigrant is beaten up again that's  no fair you're going to be discriminated  against you haven't got a chance what's  the point of sending that message out  the employer on the other hand  it's not going to make him more inclined  to hire immigrants  if anything it might make him more  negatively disposed towards immigrants  what we need to do is talk about the  success success stories everywhere i go  in canada i see lots of immigrants  employed in many different situations  yeah it is difficult it is not fair the  native speaker the native board has a  big advantage yes the native speaker  native born knows the culture knows the  language has friends connections all of  these things that you the immigrant  don't have life is unfair  okay but you came here now you've got to  try to make do  one of the  comments on my blog was from one of our  my good friend mr umberto who is a  longtime user of linguist and linkedin  stuff and he said he was standing in  line with a person from the ukraine who  claimed to have been in la and he lives  in calgary so this was in calgary this  in calgary has been there for three  years and only got six job interviews  well that's just not possible in my  opinion because three years ago in  calgary there  the job market was as tight as can be  and we have a sawmill in northern  alberta where we can't find couldn't  find people now it's completely changed  in the last six months or eight months  but in those days you couldn't find  people we brought in  uh technical people from the philippines  we hired two uh technical people  from china and they were all very well  accepted and they managed to communicate  fine so  all i'm saying is we shouldn't always be  looking for examples of discrimination  and racism and all this other stuff  people have genuine reasons for  and legitimate reasons not that that's  not to say that there aren't people out  there with prejudices and the people  with prejudices are not limited to  people in the mainstream economy in fact  probably the mainstrea